WEBVTT
1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.820
Embeddings-based
retrieval is still a very

2
00:00:01.820 --> 00:00:03.980
active area of research, and
there's a

3
00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:05.980
lot of other techniques that you
should be aware of.

4
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:09.080
For example, you can fine-tune
the embedding model

5
00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:11.720
directly using the same type of
data as we used in the

6
00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:12.960
embeddings adapters lab.

7
00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:15.180
Additionally, recently there's
been some really good

8
00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:19.540
results published in fine-tuning the
LLM itself to expect

9
00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:22.340
retrieved results and reason
about them.

10
00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:24.680
You can see some
of the papers highlighted here.

11
00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:28.140
Additionally, you could experiment
with a more

12
00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:30.760
complicated embedding adapter
model using a full-blown

13
00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:32.960
neural network or even
a transformer layer.

14
00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:35.000
Similarly, you can use
a more complex

15
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:38.840
relevance modeling model
rather than just using the cross-encoder

16
00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:42.720
as we described in the lab. And
finally, an often overlooked piece

17
00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:46.440
is that the quality of retrieved
results often depends

18
00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:48.960
on the way that your data
is chunked before it's stored

19
00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:50.460
in the retrieval system itself.

20
00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:52.600
There's a lot of
experimentation going on

21
00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:55.780
right now about using deep models
including transformers for

22
00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:57.800
optimal and intelligent
chunking. And

23
00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:58.820
that wraps up the course.

24
00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.580
In this course, we covered the
basics of retrieval

25
00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:04.060
augmented generation using
embeddings-based retrieval.

26
00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:09.180
We looked at how we can use LLMs to
augment and enhance our queries to

27
00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:10.620
produce better retrieval results.

28
00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:16.140
We looked at how we can use a cross-encoder
model for re-ranking to

29
00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:18.460
score the retrieved results
for relevancy.

30
00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:20.800
And we looked at how we
can train an

31
00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:22.640
embedding adapter using
data from human

32
00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:25.740
feedback about relevancy
to improve our query results.

33
00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:29.240
Finally, we covered some of
the most exciting work that's

34
00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:32.100
ongoing right now in the research
literature around improving

35
00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.440
retrieval for AI
applications.

36
00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:37.340
Thanks for joining the course and we're
really looking forward to

37
00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:38.320
